" (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1787297/000155837023009256/pasg-20230331x10q.htm)\nciteturn675search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; In August 2022, we dosed the first patient in our upliFT-D trial. ... We have two clinical product candidates, PBKR03 and PBML04, for which, in order to reduce operating expenses, we have stopped further clinical development and are exploring strategic alternatives for these assets. ... Our preclinical data in ARSA-/- mice and in NHPs support the ability of PBML04 administration into CSF to result in dose-dependent increases in brain\n\n​\n\nIn August 2022, we dosed the first patient in our upliFT-D trial.\n\n​\n\nWe expect to report initial safety and biomarker data from patients in Cohort 1 in the second half of 2023.\n\n​\n\nThe FDA has granted ODD and Fast Track Designation to PBFT02 for the treatment of FTD-GRN and the European Commission granted Orphan designation for PBFT02.\n\n​\n\nThrough our manufacturing partners, we have manufactured the PBFT02 clinical supply and have established a clinical supply chain to support global clinical trials.\n\nOther Clinical Product Candidates\n\nWe have two clinical product candidates, PBKR03 and PBML04, for which, in order to reduce operating expenses, we have stopped further clinical development and are exploring strategic alternatives for these assets.\n\n​\n\nPBKR03 utilizes a proprietary, next-generation AAVhu68 capsid to deliver to the brain and peripheral tissues a functional GALC gene encoding the hydrolytic enzyme galactosylceramidase to treat Krabbe disease. Krabbe disease is an autosomal recessive lysosomal storage disease caused by mutations in the GALC gene, which provides instructions for making an enzyme called galactosylceramidase, which breaks down certain fats, including galactosylceramide and psychosine. This results in the accumulation of galactolipids such as psychosine, resulting in widespread death of myelin-producing cells in the CNS and in the peripheral nervous system, or PNS. We have an active IND from the FDA and approved CTAs in multiple countries for PBKR03 to support our GALax-C Trial, an international, multi-center, open-label, single-arm Phase 1/2 clinical trial of PBKR03 in patients with a diagnosis of infantile Krabbe disease. In March 2022, we dosed the first patient in our GALax-C Trial. In November 2022, we announced plans to stop further clinical development of PBKR03 in order to reduce operating expenses, and are exploring strategic alternatives for this asset.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n0001558370-23-018686 (https://s203.q4cdn.com/877117837/files/doc_financials/2023/q3/247ca6e3-f51c-4446-9f2c-c489f8389b6f.pdf)\nciteturn675search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; In August 2022, we dosed the first patient in our upliFT-D trial. ... We have two clinical product candidates, PBKR03 and PBML04, for which, in order to reduce operating expenses, we have stopped further clinical development and are exploring strategic alternatives for these assets.\n\nWe have an active IND from the FDA and approved CTAs in multiple countries for PBFT02, which allows us to proceed with our upliFT-D Trial, an international, multi-center, open-label, single-arm Phase 1/2 clinical trial of PBFT02 in patients with a diagnosis of early symptomatic FTD-*GRN*.\n\nIn August 2022, we dosed the first patient in our upliFT-D trial.\n\nWe expect to report initial safety and biomarker data from three patients in Cohort 1 in the fourth quarter of 2023.\n\nThe FDA has granted ODD and Fast Track Designation to PBFT02 for the treatment of FTD-*GRN* and the European Commission granted Orphan designation for PBFT02.\n\nThrough our manufacturing partners, we have manufactured the PBFT02 clinical supply and have established a clinical supply chain to support global clinical trials.\n\n### *Other Clinical Product Candidates*\n\nWe have two clinical product candidates, PBKR03 and PBML04, for which, in order to reduce operating expenses, we have stopped further clinical development and are exploring strategic alternatives for these assets.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTable of Contents (https://www.annualreports.com/HostedData/AnnualReportArchive/p/NASDAQ_PASG_2022.pdf)\nciteturn675search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; In August 2022, we dosed the first patient in our upliFT-D trial. ... We have two clinical product candidates, PBKR03 and PBML04, for which, in order to reduce operating expenses, we have\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1787297/000155837023002833/pasg-20221231x10k.htm)\nciteturn675search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; In August 2022, we dosed the first patient in our upliFT-D trial. ... We have two clinical product candidates, PBKR03 and PBML04, for which, in order to reduce operating expenses, we have stopped further clinical development and are exploring strategic alternatives for these assets. ... Our preclinical data in ARSA-/- mice and in NHPs support the ability of PBML04 administration into CSF to result in dose-dependent increases in brain and CSF levels of functional human ARSA enzyme, leading to improved biochemical, histopathological, behavioral, survival endpoints, and with no safety or toxicity signs up to the highest tested dose in NHPs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1787297/000155837023013489/pasg-20230630x10q.htm)\nciteturn675search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; In July 2023, we dosed the first patient at dose 3. ... We have two clinical product candidates, PBKR03 and PBML04, for which, in order to reduce operating expenses, we have 